# Project Writeup (Informal Draft)

## Background

My interest in this project actually comes from two pretty different places.
The game theory side comes from video games — I've always been drawn to
situations where individual decisions interact with what everyone else is
doing, and where the "best" move depends on what you expect others to do.
The swarm intelligence side comes from a video I saw of ants versus humans
trying to fit an I-beam-shaped object through a narrow slot: the ants beat
the humans at it, and that stuck with me. It's a simple demonstration of a
decentralized group outperforming individually "smarter" agents just
through how they coordinate, and I wanted to understand why and how that
works.

Coming into this project I had a decent Python background, but I didn't
know much about contextual bandits or how to actually design reward
systems for a multi-agent, game-theoretic setting. Learning both of those
— what a contextual bandit actually is and does, and how reward design
changes what a system learns to do — has been most of the technical
learning curve so far.

## Motivation

Honestly, the straightforward answer is that I find this genuinely
interesting, and I like the process of learning something, seeing
progress, and debugging errors along the way — for me, debugging is part
of how I can actually tell whether real learning (both the system's and my
own) is happening, rather than just producing something that looks right
on the surface.

Beyond that, I want this to become something resume-worthy — a real
artifact I can talk about in an interview, not just a class project — and
I see it as a stepping stone toward a specific career direction I'm
working towards. Ideally, the end result is a working, genuinely optimized
system, not just something that runs.

## The Core Problem

Both of those interests — game theory and swarm coordination — pointed me
toward the same kind of question, but "study swarm intelligence" on its
own is too broad to actually investigate. It doesn't pin down a mechanism,
a decision being made, or a way to measure success. The version of this
that I think is actually answerable is narrower:

> **Core problem:** In a decentralized system where independent agents
> must choose whether to contribute to a shared task (a threshold-based
> public-goods game — like the ants needing enough of them to move the
> I-beam together), does the *design of the reward signal* — specifically,
> how credit gets assigned when a group of agents jointly succeeds —
> change whether a learning population discovers cooperative strategies
> like recruiting help, versus just acting in its own immediate
> self-interest?

I scoped this down in three deliberate ways:

1. **One game mechanic, not an open-ended environment.** The task
   structure is simple and fixed: a quota of unique contributors has to be
   reached before a deadline. This is a well-studied game-theoretic
   structure (a threshold public-goods game), not a sprawling simulated
   world with too many moving parts to draw a clean conclusion from.
2. **One learning mechanism, not a comparison of algorithms.** Agents use
   a single, well-understood learning method — a shared LinUCB contextual
   bandit — rather than me trying to compare five different algorithms at
   once. The reward signal is the variable I'm actually studying, not the
   algorithm itself.
3. **One specific, identified reward flaw — not "improve the reward
   somehow."** The concrete question is whether fixing two credit-
   assignment problems I found — an arbitrary "whoever happens to complete
   the quota gets the entire bonus" rule, and zero reward for successfully
   recruiting help — actually changes learned behavior. That's a
   falsifiable, measurable question, not just a vague direction.

## Why This Scope (Not Broader, Not Narrower)

Going broader (a general "swarm intelligence" study) risks turning into a
survey rather than an actual finding — there'd be too many free variables
(agent count, environment design, learning algorithm, communication
structure) to say anything clean about any one of them. Fixing the
environment and the learning algorithm, and varying only the reward
design, isolates a single factor I can actually test.

Going narrower — say, a purely theoretical derivation of "fair" credit
assignment on paper — would avoid the messiness of a live simulation, but
it wouldn't test whether a *learning* system actually discovers the right
behavior under real conditions: imperfect information, timing pressure,
and the exploration/exploitation tradeoff. Keeping it empirical, using an
actual running multi-agent simulation, keeps the project grounded in
whether learning really happens, not just whether a fairer reward function
can be defined in the abstract.

In one line: fix the game and the learner, vary the reward, measure the
behavior. Narrow enough to actually finish, broad enough to say something
real about cooperation in decentralized systems.

## Direction / Approach

- **Environment:** a population of agents (currently 50) with shared
  roles, who receive task notifications and choose to ignore, reply, or
  recruit a peer ("collab") in response.
- **Learning:** a single shared LinUCB contextual bandit. I chose this
  over full reinforcement learning because reward in this environment is
  close to immediate, and the multi-agent setting is non-stationary (every
  agent is learning at the same time as every other agent) — conditions
  where a bandit is more stable and sample-efficient than a bootstrapped
  RL method, though it comes at the cost of not being able to plan over
  long horizons.
- **The reward intervention I'm actually studying:** a credit-assignment
  redesign inspired by COMA (Counterfactual Multi-Agent Policy Gradients)
  — not a full implementation of COMA's centralized critic, but borrowing
  its core idea. Specifically: splitting the completion reward evenly
  across every contributor instead of giving it entirely to whichever
  agent happened to complete the quota last, and adding a reward for
  recruitment that only pays off if the recruited peer actually helps.
- **What "success" looks like for this project:** evidence, from comparing
  runs under the old vs. new reward design, of whether recruitment
  behavior is used more, is used more effectively, or leads to higher
  task-completion rates — with a real explanation for *why*, tied
  specifically to the credit-assignment mechanism, not just "the numbers
  went up."
- **Limitations I'm being upfront about from the start:** how an agent
  picks who to recruit is currently a fixed rule (prefer a known peer
  whose role matches), not itself learned yet — that's a planned next
  step. The reward fix is COMA-*inspired*, not a full implementation.
  Results so far come from dozens of simulation runs, not yet a
  large-scale statistical sample.

## Debugging as Part of the Process

Since I mentioned that debugging is how I actually tell whether real
learning is happening — both mine and the system's — here's a concrete
example from this project so far. I originally had role-matching as
optional (an agent's role only gave a reward bonus, it didn't restrict who
could help). When I made role-matching strictly enforced, to make the task
environment harder and more realistic, it exposed a bug that had been
sitting there invisibly: role assignment had accidentally been giving
almost every agent a unique role, meaning some tasks' quotas were
mathematically impossible to reach no matter what any agent did.

After fixing that, task completion jumped to 100% and recruitment usage
dropped to zero — because it turned out the system was already notifying
every eligible agent directly, so there was nothing left for recruitment
to meaningfully do. That forced a second fix: deliberately under-notifying
eligible agents, so that reaching the full quota actually depends on
agents choosing to recruit each other.

Neither of those was something I planned for in advance — I found both by
running the simulation, looking at the results, and asking "why is this
number what it is." That trial-and-error process is a big part of what
I've actually learned so far, and I think it's worth including here rather
than writing this up as if the design was right from the start.

## Next Steps

- Run a larger batch of simulations (aiming for dozens more runs) to get a
  statistically stronger read on whether the new reward design actually
  improves cooperative behavior over the old one.
- Compare completion rates, recruitment usage, and the bandit's own
  learned weights between the old and new reward schemes directly.
- If time allows, extend recruitment from a fixed rule into something the
  agents actually learn (i.e. who tends to be worth recruiting), which
  would tie back more directly into the game-theoretic side of the
  project.

